f 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192789 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating Probabilities With Piecewise PDFs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192789 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192789 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "17408f35-ef57-5eb2-8cb6-3a946194d2d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192912 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192912 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Probabilities With Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192912 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186193666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192912 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192912 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192912 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "3166e137-4d3f-5719-abca-e284bb2446a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193389 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193389 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86913" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193389 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193389 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute $P(2<X<8)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{50}x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 10, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193389 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d522d4b-cd13-5ffd-9293-3255ca27b6de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193389 #edb/ref 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<8) & ={\\int }_{2}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{8}\\frac{1}{50}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{100}{x}^{2}]}_{2}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193611 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193611 #edb/ref 1061 41078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193611 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a3e6cf46-1327-5a3a-8953-f8f5c744aa8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193611 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193665 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193665 #edb/ref 1061 41927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193665 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f94672ce-3841-5241-8adb-0cd157483370" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193665 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193666 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193666 #edb/ref 1061 41930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193666 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "167eafc7-5e80-5952-9c5c-541b7d78828f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193666 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193667 #edb/ref 1061 41936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193667 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c5ab4c4d-fb97-5d7e-a711-dca2df3d7318" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193667 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193886 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the **probability density function** (or **pdf**) of a continuous random variable $X$ defined over a set $S$ is a function $f(x)$ that satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nThe probability that $X$ lies in the interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nThis probability can be interpreted as the area bounded between $f(x)$ and the $x$-axis over the interval $x\\in [a,b],$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e6c21e69a0dbd15ce32d18c2e74d8fcab0276e82bc55b354eaa5140adff610e6.png)\n\nAlso, remember that for continuous random variables, we get the same answer whether or not we include the endpoints of an interval $[a,b].$ In other words,\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = P(a < X < b)  = P(a < X \\leq b) = P(a \\leq X < b).\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll get some practice at computing probabilities with continuous random variables." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193886 #edb/ref 1065 6926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193886 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a6c7afdc-da68-55d5-aaa4-9ff1c77b6740" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193886 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272556 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{9}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272556 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272556 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "12b8b202-7725-53aa-b893-d1b69f0354a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272790 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{24}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272790 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272790 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d2809d8-203c-5893-a099-43303ed8f126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272990 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{12}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272990 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272990 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "46adcefc-4dfa-5a5c-b624-02599ee711d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273485 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{12}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273485 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273485 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "801203